{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1135","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1135","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1135\",\"parent_revision\":\"6f7a17200ab4935135914c4eeb5a3dbe3e8a5af0\",\"root_revision\":\"be155fd5e19ffb339936248803604dce5a1b4402c9b0e064a9d574317fb8dc14\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen work-backlog-item loads a groomed item (groomed=date), it proceeds to planning without checking whether the codebase has changed in ways that affect the item since grooming. This produces plans built on stale assumptions.\\n\\n**Two-phase check, inside Step 3.1** (after groomed=true confirmed, before using cached content):\\n\\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Drift detection** (is there any change?):\\nRun `git log --oneline --after=\\\"{groomed_date}\\\" --diff-filter=AMRD -- {impact_radius_files}` filtered to functional commits (commit type: feat|fix|refactor|perf|revert; OR file extension: .py/.ts/.yaml/.json). If zero functional commits: skip Phase 2, use cached content.\\n\\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Drift assessment** (does the change matter?):\\nSpawn an agent with: item description, acceptance criteria, and `git diff {groom_date_sha}..HEAD -- {impact_radius_files}`. Agent outputs one of:\\n- `FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT`: diff changes the feasibility, approach, architecture, or goals of the item \u2192 write Staleness Context section, invoke `groom-backlog-item` with diff as context\\n- `SUPERSEDED`: diff implements what the item describes \u2192 close item with commit reference\\n- `COSMETIC_ONLY`: diff does not affect item goals \u2192 treat as valid, proceed\\n\\n**AUTO_MODE**: same flow, no AskUserQuestion \u2014 decisions are evidence-derived from diff content.\\n\\n**Acceptance criteria**:\\n- [ ] Items with zero functional commits since groom date proceed without re-grooming\\n- [ ] Items with functional drift that changes goals/approach are re-groomed before planning\\n- [ ] Items whose goals were implemented by recent commits are closed as superseded\\n- [ ] AUTO_MODE runs the full assessment without interactive prompts\\n- [ ] Re-groom invocation passes the git diff as Staleness Context to groom-backlog-item\\n- [ ] After re-groom completes, RT-ICA re-runs (updated_at is now newer than RT-ICA date)\\n\\n**Depends on**: fix backlog_view groom date bug (needs reliable groomed date), #964 (for clean Impact Radius file path extraction \u2014 until #964 lands, use regex extraction from sections[\\\"Impact Radius\\\"])\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFact-Check Summary for #1135 (2026-04-01)\\n\\n| # | Claim | Verdict | Evidence |\\n|---|-------|---------|----------|\\n| 1 | `groomed` field returns date string (not `True`) after db60f078 | VERIFIED | operations.py:1885 assigns `entry[\\\"groomed\\\"] = item.groomed` \u2014 raw date string from model |\\n| 2 | `git log --oneline --after=... --diff-filter=AMRD -- {files}` syntax works | VERIFIED | Command executed in project root, returned 12 commits, no errors |\\n| 3 | File paths in `sections['Impact Radius']` of groomed items | REFUTED | Section key is `'impact radius'` (lowercase) \u2014 `sections['Impact Radius']` returns None |\\n| 4 | `groom-backlog-item` accepts args as prefix string for Staleness Context | REFUTED | Skill accepts only: title substring, section label (P0/P1/etc), or 'all' \u2014 no injection mechanism |\\n\\nTotals: 2 VERIFIED, 0 INCONCLUSIVE, 2 REFUTED\\n\\nResolutions:\\n- Claim 3: Use `sections['impact radius']` (lowercase) in implementation \u2014 corrected, AVAILABLE\\n- Claim 4: Write \\\"Staleness Context\\\" section to item via backlog_groom before invoking groom-backlog-item. Groom skill reads all sections naturally via backlog_view \u2014 no changes to groom-backlog-item needed. Design decision documented, AVAILABLE.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Add staleness detection and functional drift assessment to work-backlog-item Step 3.1\\nDate: 2026-04-01\\nGoal: Insert a two-phase codebase-drift check into Step 3.1 to prevent planning on stale groomed content\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Target file step-procedures.md exists | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst confirmed primary target\\n2. Current Step 3.1 has no staleness logic | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Explore agent + impact-analyst\\n3. groomed field returns date string after db60f078 | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-check VERIFIED, operations.py:1885\\n4. git log --after/--diff-filter=AMRD syntax works | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-check VERIFIED, executed in project root\\n5. Impact Radius section key is 'impact radius' (lowercase) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-check REFUTED\u2192RESOLVED: key is lowercase\\n6. Staleness Context injection mechanism for groom-backlog-item | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-check REFUTED\u2192RESOLVED: write section via backlog_groom before invoking groom; groom reads all sections naturally\\n7. auto-mode.md rows for staleness outcomes | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst: 3 required new rows (FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT, SUPERSEDED, COSMETIC_ONLY)\\n8. Agent spawn for Phase 2 diff assessment | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: standard Agent tool pattern\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (section key is 'impact radius' lowercase)\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (Staleness Context written as item section before groom invocation)\\n- Condition 7: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (3 explicit auto-mode.md rows scoped)\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n\\nImplementation scope:\\n- 3 required file edits: step-procedures.md (Phase 1+2 procedure), SKILL.md (Step 3.1 trigger text), auto-mode.md (3 new rows)\\n- 1 recommended: example-sessions.md (update examples to show new Step 3.1 flow)\\n- Taxonomy note: new taxonomy is for Step 3.1 only; Step 2.5 drift-check.md taxonomy is separate and unaffected\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **feat: add staleness detection and functional drift assessment to work-backlog-item step 3.1** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Staleness policy design session 2026-03-30 \u2014 auto-mode-regroom research agent\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-30\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-01)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nWithout this guard:\\n\\n1. A backlog item is groomed on date D.\\n2. Between D and today, commits land that alter the implementation approach for files in the item's impact radius.\\n3. An agent works the item loading cached groom content without awareness of those commits.\\n4. The agent executes against a stale plan.\\n\\nTo reproduce: find any groomed item whose `groomed` date is older than 14 days, run `git log --oneline --after=\\\"{groomed_date}\\\" --diff-filter=AMRD -- {impact_radius_files}`, observe functional commits the working agent would never see.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nP1\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\nAffected: every agent that works a groomed backlog item where functional commits have landed since the groom date.\\n\\n**If this ships**: detects functional drift before loading cached content, re-grooms with diff as context, or closes if goal was already implemented. Planning quality protected for all items.\\n\\n**If gap remains**: agents produce plans against stale designs. Failure is silent \u2014 output is misaligned with current codebase; discovered at review or integration test time.\\n\\nSeverity scales with backlog age and commit frequency. Items groomed during active development sprints are highest risk.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] **Zero functional commits \u2192 no re-groom**: When `git log --oneline --after=\\\"{groomed_date}\\\" --diff-filter=AMRD -- {impact_radius_files}` returns no lines matching functional commit criteria, agent proceeds with cached content without invoking groom-backlog-item.\\n- [ ] **Functional drift \u2192 re-groom before planning**: When Phase 1 finds functional commits and Phase 2 agent returns FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT, agent writes 'Staleness Context' section to item via `backlog_groom` (containing diff summary), then invokes groom-backlog-item before any planning.\\n- [ ] **Superseded \u2192 close without planning**: When Phase 2 returns SUPERSEDED, agent calls `backlog_close` with commit reference(s) and stops.\\n- [ ] **AUTO_MODE runs assessment without prompts**: When AUTO_MODE=true, Phase 1 and Phase 2 run and branch to FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT/SUPERSEDED/COSMETIC_ONLY without interactive choices.\\n- [ ] **Re-groom receives diff as Staleness Context**: The `backlog_groom` call writes a Staleness Context section containing `git diff {groom_date_sha}..HEAD -- {impact_radius_files}` output summarised as context.\\n- [ ] **RT-ICA re-runs after re-groom**: After groom-backlog-item updates `updated_at` to today, Step 3.1 re-evaluates RT-ICA condition and finds it satisfied, causing RT-ICA to run against fresh content.\\n\\n### Skills\\n\\n- `/dh:work-backlog-item` \u2014 skill being modified; read step-procedures.md Step 3.1, auto-mode.md, SKILL.md before editing\\n- `/dh:groom-backlog-item` \u2014 invoked by FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT branch; understand interface and how Staleness Context is consumed\\n- Backlog MCP (`mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom`) \u2014 used to write Staleness Context section before invoking groom skill; section key is lowercase ('staleness context')\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nplugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/step-procedures.md   [REQUIRED \u2014 add Phase 1+2 procedure to Step 3.1]\\nplugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md                        [REQUIRED \u2014 update Step 3.1 trigger text]\\nplugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/auto-mode.md         [REQUIRED \u2014 add 3 rows: FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT, SUPERSEDED, COSMETIC_ONLY]\\nplugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/example-sessions.md  [RECOMMENDED \u2014 update examples to show new staleness flow]\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- #1134 \u2014 groomed date fix (DONE): ensures `groomed=date` frontmatter is written correctly so Phase 1 git log has a reliable `--after` date\\n- #964 \u2014 YAML migration (DONE): ensures backlog items are in the format that `backlog_groom` reads/writes sections against\\n\\nNo open dependencies.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n3 story points\\n\\nRationale: All 6 ACs are fully specified with exact commands and decision branches. Three skill files require editing (step-procedures.md, SKILL.md, auto-mode.md) plus one recommended (example-sessions.md). No new MCP tools, no source code changes. The procedure logic (two git commands + one agent spawn + three-way branch) is well-bounded.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\nIssue Classification: missing-guardrail\\n\\nThe item adds a staleness detection gate to Step 3.1 to catch functional drift in Impact Radius files between grooming and execution. Without this check, agents proceed on potentially outdated context. This is a missing quality gate, not a new feature or defect.\\n\\nNo root-cause analysis required for missing-guardrail type.\\n\\n## Code Producers Primary Target\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/step-procedures.md` \u2014 Step 3.1 body. Anchor `#step-3-1-groom-check` must be preserved (SKILL.md links to it). Add Phase 1 git log filter, Phase 2 agent spawn, classification enum, and action table.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Systems Inventory\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| File | Role | Change Required |\\n|------|------|-----------------|\\n| step-procedures.md | Primary target \u2014 procedure body | Yes \u2014 add Phase 1 + Phase 2 |\\n| SKILL.md | Workflow description | Yes \u2014 update Step 3.1 trigger text |\\n| auto-mode.md | AUTO_MODE substitution table | Yes \u2014 add 3 explicit rows |\\n| example-sessions.md | Usage examples | Recommended \u2014 update examples |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Configuration Ci\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- None identified.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Decision Required\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe new taxonomy (FUNCTIONAL_DRIFT / SUPERSEDED / COSMETIC_ONLY) in Step 3.1 is distinct from Step 2.5 drift-check.md taxonomy (Scope change / Partial fix / New callers / File moved / No impact). These address different checkpoints and do not need unification \u2014 document this explicitly in the new Step 3.1 text to avoid confusion.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Code Consumers\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- None (SKILL.md is not source code, no callers to break)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Artifact Manifest\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:begin -->\\n| Type | Path | Status | Agent | Created |\\n|------|------|--------|-------|---------|\\n| feature-context | plan/feature-context-staleness-detection-work-backlog-item.md | current | discovery | 2026-04-01T14:14:04.917309+00:00 |\\n| architect | .tmp/scratch/reports/2026-04-01-architect-1135.md | current | general-purpose | 2026-04-01T14:30:37.781118+00:00 |\\n| task-plan | plan/P1135-staleness-detection-step-3-1.yaml | current |  | 2026-04-01T14:36:40Z |\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:end -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Agent Instructions\\